WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
This is The Spread, your energy
markets briefing for Monday,

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.520
the twenty-third of November.

3
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.640
This episode is written and read with AI.

4
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.480
Oil posted another weekly loss even
as tankers kept getting hit near

5
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:17.600
Oman. Supply is plentiful, US sour
crude has lost its war premium,

6
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.160
and Gulf barrels are flowing into China.

7
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.133
Meanwhile the Houthis have told
ships to leave Saudi Red Sea ports,

8
00:00:24.187 --> 00:00:27.680
which puts Yanbu, the Hormuz
bypass, in their sights.

9
00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:32.160
On the markets. Brent fell about five
percent last week to around ninety-one

10
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.080
dollars, according to OilPrice dot
com, and Rigzone notes that was its

11
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.080
third straight weekly loss.

12
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.760
January West Texas Intermediate
lost more than five percent.

13
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.680
Early Friday, Brent was at
ninety-two dollars twenty,

14
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.040
and West Texas Intermediate
at eighty-two dollars seventy.

15
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.840
Baker Hughes counted six more US oil rigs,

16
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.707
taking the total to four
hundred and sixty-nine.

17
00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:56.053
The biggest shift is in sour crude.

18
00:00:56.107 --> 00:00:58.827
The price of US sour
grades has fallen hard,

19
00:00:58.853 --> 00:01:02.120
Bloomberg and Rigzone reported,
as barrels from Venezuela,

20
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.840
Canada and the Middle East surge and
refiners find they have plenty to

21
00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.240
choose from. Neither report
puts a size on the move.

22
00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.560
China shows the pull most clearly.

23
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.200
Imports from Gulf producers other
than Iran reached nineteen point seven

24
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.360
million tonnes in October, according to
a South China Morning Post calculation

25
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.253
from Chinese customs data.

26
00:01:21.307 --> 00:01:24.187
That was up forty-two point
four percent from September,

27
00:01:24.213 --> 00:01:26.427
and the highest since the war began.

28
00:01:26.453 --> 00:01:31.160
Gulf producers cut prices to win buyers
while freight sat at record highs.

29
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.520
For sour-heavy refiners on
the US Gulf Coast and in Asia,

30
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.160
that's a reversal. The scarcity
that squeezed their margins for most

31
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.240
of the year has turned into
a choice of cheap barrels.

32
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.040
And as medium-sour supply returns,
the premium for light-sweet crude

33
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.613
could widen again.

34
00:01:46.667 --> 00:01:49.400
Out in the Gulf, the
attacks haven't stopped.

35
00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.200
On Friday a tanker was struck by a
projectile in the Strait of Hormuz

36
00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.800
and caught fire, gCaptain reported.

37
00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.520
On Saturday, according to Reuters,
an outbound tanker was hit on the

38
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.440
stern by an unidentified projectile
about eleven nautical miles north

39
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.840
of Khasab, in Oman. A separate vessel
reportedly struck a mine east of

40
00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:08.840
the strait.

41
00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.280
None of these reports names the attacker.

42
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.720
Bloomberg's tally shows attacks
peaked in October at the highest level

43
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.040
of the war and have continued
almost daily this month.

44
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.093
Crude volumes through Hormuz
have dipped in recent days,

45
00:02:21.147 --> 00:02:23.960
though the reports don't say
whether the attacks are the cause.

46
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.640
Indian refiners are feeling
it on the freight side,

47
00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:31.280
Rigzone reported. They're rushing to
lock in tankers on long-term charters.

48
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:35.680
Turning to the Red Sea. The Saudi-led
coalition says it struck the Yemeni

49
00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.440
port of Hodeidah on Friday to
destroy Houthi capabilities,

50
00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.600
according to gCaptain. The Houthis
responded by ordering ships to leave

51
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.320
Saudi Red Sea ports, warning airlines
away from Saudi airspace and promising

52
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:49.200
new strikes.

53
00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.280
Earlier on Friday, media reported
Houthi strikes on Riyadh,

54
00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.480
hitting the financial district, the
sovereign wealth fund's headquarters

55
00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.320
and a petrochemicals company.

56
00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.480
Those reports come from media
only, with no Saudi confirmation.

57
00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.080
Brent briefly rose to ninety-two
dollars seventy on the news.

58
00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.240
What makes this matter is Yanbu.

59
00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.320
It's the Red Sea outlet Saudi
Arabia uses to get around Hormuz,

60
00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.000
so a credible threat there lands on the
very route that has carried diverted

61
00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:20.160
barrels. So far, no effect on
loadings has been reported.

62
00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.120
On Iran, the blockade is being tested.

63
00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.200
Last week Washington widened its naval
blockade of Iranian ports to cover

64
00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:31.680
ship-to-ship transfers. Since then,
a cargo of Iranian crude has moved

65
00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.080
inside the Gulf from a sanctioned
Suezmax to an unsanctioned medium-range

66
00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.240
tanker, according to United Against
Nuclear Iran and the tracking firm

67
00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.400
Kpler. The first of those is
an anti-Iran advocacy group.

68
00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:48.840
OilPrice dot com reads the transfer
as Iran preparing to restart exports.

69
00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:50.920
Tehran hasn't confirmed that.

70
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.240
Washington, for its part, is
leaning on the transit hubs.

71
00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.560
Treasury officials met regulators in
Singapore and Malaysia to warn them

72
00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:01.280
about sanctions compliance,
gCaptain reported.

73
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.200
Both countries handle transfers
of Iranian crude headed for China.

74
00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:09.040
The Treasury's sanctions office
also issued new Iran designations on

75
00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.840
Friday, though the notice names no one.

76
00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:17.120
Separately, Iran again refused UN
inspectors access to its nuclear sites.

77
00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.800
Saudi Arabia and Japan announced
a storage deal on Friday.

78
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.400
Japan will buy up to eight million
barrels of Saudi crude to rebuild

79
00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.680
the national reserve it drew
down during the Hormuz crisis,

80
00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.680
if market conditions are favourable.

81
00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.680
In return, Saudi Arabia gets temporary
use of a further sixteen million

82
00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:37.600
barrels of Japanese storage, on
top of what it already holds there.

83
00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.240
Bloomberg and Rigzone report it
has begun refilling those tanks.

84
00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:45.680
Over to European gas. Storage stood
at sixty-nine point eight percent

85
00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.280
full on Wednesday and sixty-nine
point two percent on Saturday,

86
00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:51.360
according to Gas Infrastructure Europe.

87
00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:55.360
Net withdrawals already equal about
eleven percent of the gas injected

88
00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.760
this summer. That summer total was
fifty-one point two billion cubic

89
00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:00.800
metres.

90
00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.200
Norway is partly offsetting the draw.

91
00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.080
It sold eleven billion
cubic metres in October,

92
00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:08.800
according to the Norwegian
Offshore Directorate.

93
00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.280
That's up about two
billion from September,

94
00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:15.320
or roughly three hundred and
fifty-six million cubic metres a day.

95
00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:16.840
A few more things.

96
00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:21.360
Vladimir Putin ruled out negotiating
after Ukraine's strikes on the Crimean

97
00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:25.200
Bridge, and Russian missiles hit
Kyiv's infrastructure on Friday.

98
00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.760
In the Gulf of Finland, a Russian
military-linked cargo ship burned after

99
00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:31.560
a suspected drone strike.

100
00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:36.080
India's demand for liquefied petroleum
gas reached two point six million

101
00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.160
tonnes in October, an eight-month
high, according to government data.

102
00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:44.160
The US national average for gasoline
was three dollars seventy a gallon

103
00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:46.520
on Friday, according to AAA.

104
00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.200
And in Australia, Inpex approved the
next offshore phase at its Ichthys

105
00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.360
liquefied natural gas plant.

106
00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.760
The work is meant to
hold output at plateau,

107
00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.880
which is nine point three
million tonnes a year.

108
00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:03.200
What to watch next. Daily EU gas
storage readings run through the third

109
00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.520
of December. On Wednesday the EIA
publishes its weekly report for the

110
00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.600
week to the twentieth of November.

111
00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:12.800
Distillate stocks stood at about
one hundred and five million barrels

112
00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.880
a week earlier. The following report,
for the week to the twenty-seventh,

113
00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:18.760
comes on the second of December.

114
00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:23.920
Further out, there's Guyana's first
Uaru cargo and Pakistan's first tender

115
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.480
cargo in mid-December, the EU ban on
long-term Russian liquefied natural

116
00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:32.640
gas from January, and the end of
Russia's diesel export ban that same

117
00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:33.400
month.

118
00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:37.520
That's the brief. Sources for everything
you heard are in the show notes.
